							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;"><div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=3342.msg37556#msg37556">Quote from: Blind Sage on September 26, 2007, 04:07:04 pm</a></div><div class="quote">Backpacker was originally a term for somebody that was really deep into hip hop.&nbsp; It came around in the early to mid-90s.&nbsp; It was also generally an East coast term.&nbsp; It was used because a lot of MCs used mass transit since they didn&#039;t own their own rides, and therefore they always had a backpack to carry around their rhyme book(s) and any product they had to sell.&nbsp; They were also frequently assumed to have dope skills. Backpackers, being from the East coast (yeah, yeah, I know not everybody was East coast, but that&#039;s where it originates), were usually very critical of West Coast music, and anything in general that didn&#039;t have that East coast grimey sound to some degree.&nbsp; In the late &#039;90s some folks started turning this on its head.&nbsp; As hip hop gained popularity and a lot of folks started listening to more hip hop on the radio that was often dumbed down, these same Backpackers, around the country, would often attack &quot;mainstream&quot; or &quot;commercial&quot; hip hop as garbage.&nbsp; So, a lot of folks started using backpacker as a dis, since those cats attacked their music, folks started attacking back.&nbsp; So, then East coast flavored music with complicated flows and abstract or philosophically minded artists started being labeled backpack in a negative way, by those who prefered other styles of hip hop.&nbsp; Backpack has since evolved to imply a narrow, elitist, self-important attitude and perspective on hip hop.<br /></div><br />wow! that&#039;s a very involved and ridiculous insult which does not particularly contribute toward my love for humanity</div>
						</td>
